/-!
# Pairs of morphisms of the fppf site of a stack, and the base data they amount to

Condition (2)(c) of **Stacks 0GMS** — equivalently condition (3) of **Stacks 0GN4** — asks, for
two objects `x`, `x'` of a site lying in a distinguished set of objects, for a finite family `V_i`
in that set with morphisms `p_i : V_i ⟶ x` and `q_i : V_i ⟶ x'` for which
`∐_i h_{V_i}^# ⟶ h_x^# ⨯ h_{x'}^#` is a surjection of sheaves of sets. For the fppf site of a
category fibred in groupoids over the big fppf category of a scheme, that sheaf-level condition
has already been reduced to a condition on the *hom-sets* of the site by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_covering`: every pair
of morphisms `α : y ⟶ x`, `β : y ⟶ x'` must factor, after passing to a covering of `y`, through
one `(p_i, q_i)`.

This file carries that reduction down to data living in the *base* category of the site, in two
independent steps.

* **The covering may be taken to be generated by one morphism, produced downstairs.** A pair
  `(α, β)` that factors through a single `g : w ⟶ y` factors over the whole sieve generated by
  `g`, and that sieve covers as soon as some covering sieve of the scheme `y` lies over factors
  through the image of `g`. This is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_singleton_lift` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_base_covering`.
* **The factorisation itself is base data plus one condition.** Relative to a fixed
  `(V, p_V, q_V)`, a pair of morphisms `α : y ⟶ x`, `β : y ⟶ x'` is the same thing as a morphism
  `c : p(y) ⟶ p(V)` of the base over which `α` factors through `p_V`, subject to the single
  condition that the resulting factorisation also carries `q_V` to `β`. This is
  `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.homEquivBase` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.homPairEquivBase`, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_base_hom` is the
  form of the condition that results.

It then rewrites the same reduction in the language the source of Stacks 0GMS argues in, namely
the 2-fibre product `U ×_{x, 𝒳, x'} U'` of the slices `x` and `x'` represent. A pair of
morphisms out of an object of the site *is* an object of that 2-fibre product — a quadruple of
Stacks 0040 — by the absolute dictionary of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/TwoFibreProduct/TwoYonedaPairs.lean`, and the total category of the
2-fibre product *of algebraic stacks* at the two 2-Yoneda 1-morphisms is the category of those
quadruples on the nose
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.obj_twoFibreProductAlgebraicStack_objectMorphism`). Condition (2)(c)
therefore follows from a single quadruple `Q` which dominates every quadruple locally, in either
of two forms — with the covering taken upstairs
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack`
`.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_quadruple_dominating`)
or downstairs, which is the shape **Stacks 06QA** produces
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack`
`.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_quadruple_dominating_base`).

## Main definitions

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.homEquivBase`: for a strongly cartesian
  `k : V ⟶ x`, the factorisations of a morphism `α : y ⟶ x` through `k` are exactly the
  factorisations of `p(α)` through `p(k)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.homPairEquivBase`: the same for a *pair* of morphisms, the extra
  condition on the base morphism being that the factorisation it induces matches the second
  morphism of the pair.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.twoFibreProductObjects`: the 2-fibre product
  `U ×_{x, 𝒳, x'} U'` of the slices two objects of the fppf site represent, with
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.quadObj`, `.quadFst`, `.quadSnd` and `.quadMap` for the object of the
  site a quadruple classifies, the two morphisms out of it, and the functoriality of all three.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.objectMorphismAlgebraicStacks`: the 2-Yoneda 1-morphism of an object
  of the fppf site, read in the 2-category of algebraic stacks.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.eq_of_map_eq`: two factorisations of one morphism
  through a strongly cartesian morphism with the same image agree.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_singleton_lift`:
  condition (2)(c) of Stacks 0GMS for the fppf site, from a factorisation over a single morphism
  with a covering singleton.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_base_covering`: the
  same with the covering singleton replaced by a covering sieve of the base.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_base_hom`: the same
  again with the factorisation replaced by base data through `homPairEquivBase`, so that the
  hypothesis mentions no morphism of the total category except the one covering morphism.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_quadruple_of_hom_pair`: every pair of morphisms of the fppf
  site out of one object is classified by an object of `U ×_{x, 𝒳, x'} U'` lying over the scheme
  that object lies over.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack`
  `.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_quadruple_dominating`
  and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack`
  `.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_quadruple_dominating_base`:
  condition (2)(c) of Stacks 0GMS for the fppf site, for the one-member family a quadruple `Q`
  classifies, from the hypothesis that `Q` dominates every quadruple after a covering — taken
  upstairs in the first and in the big fppf category over `S` in the second.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_covering_quadMap_of_exists_quadruple_dominating_base` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_covering_hom_pair_of_exists_quadruple_dominating`: the
  site-level content of those two, the second being the hom-set form of condition (2)(c) that
  `isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_covering` consumes. They are stated apart
  from the sheaf-level conclusions because, mentioning only the hom-sets, they transport along an
  equivalence of sites across a universe gap — which is what the check of Stacks 0GMS case (2) on
  an equi-universe model of the fppf site needs.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.obj_twoFibreProductAlgebraicStack_objectMorphism`: the total category
  of the 2-fibre product of algebraic stacks at the two 2-Yoneda 1-morphisms of two objects of
  the fppf site is the category of quadruples, with no transport.

## What is proved and what remains

Nothing here is a statement of the Stacks project and no `@[stacks]` attribute is carried.
Condition (3) of Stacks 0GN4 keeps its site on
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_covering`, and
Stacks 0GMS case (2) keeps its single site on
`CategoryTheory.Sheaf.exists_set_isLocallySurjective_and_isQuasiCompact_prod_self`; the results
below are inputs to those, not assertions of the source.

In particular condition (2)(c) for the fppf site of a quasi-compact and quasi-separated algebraic
stack is **not** proved here. Writing `U` for the affine scheme `x` lies over and `U'` for the one
`x'` lies over, the source produces the family of condition (2)(c) — a single member — by covering
the algebraic space `U ×_{x, 𝒳, x'} U'` with an affine scheme. The dictionary between that
2-fibre product and the pairs of morphisms of the site, which the module documentation of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/ColimitsOfCohomology.lean` names as missing, **is now
available**: its relative half is `homPairEquivBase` and its absolute half — the identification
of the quadruples with the pairs of morphisms out of an object over the base of the quadruple —
is `StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/TwoFibreProduct/TwoYonedaPairs.lean`, whose object and morphism
parts are used above through `quadObj`, `quadFst`, `quadSnd`, `quadMap`,
`quadMap_comp_quadFst`, `quadMap_comp_quadSnd` and `exists_quadruple_of_hom_pair`. And the
2-fibre product the source covers is, on the nose, the category of those quadruples, by
`obj_twoFibreProductAlgebraicStack_objectMorphism`.

What remains for condition (2)(c) is therefore exactly **one step**: to produce a quadruple `Q`
satisfying the hypothesis of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack`
`.isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_quadruple_dominating_base`, that is, a
quadruple lying over an affine scheme which every other quadruple is dominated by after a
covering of the scheme it lies over. That is the statement that a surjective, flat, locally
finitely presented 1-morphism onto `U ×_{x, 𝒳, x'} U'` is locally essentially surjective, which
is **Stacks 06QA**, `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_fppfCovering_fibre_lift`, applied to an
affine smooth presentation of that 2-fibre product; the pattern is the one
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_isAffineObject_cover_nonempty_hom` follows for condition (1). That
step is taken in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/ColimitsOfCohomologyProductAffineCover.lean`, where
condition (2)(c) is proved, for an algebraic stack quasi-separated over a quasi-separated base
scheme, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_fin_affineObjects_isLocallySurjective_prodLift`. Its two geometric
ingredients are

* an affine surjective smooth presentation of a quasi-compact algebraic stack, by **Stacks 04YC**,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isQuasiCompactStack_iff_hasAffineSmoothPresentation`;
* quasi-compactness of the 2-fibre product, by **Stacks 075S** part (3),
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isQuasiCompactStack_and_isQuasiSeparatedOver_twoFibreProduct`, which is
  where quasi-separatedness of the stack enters.

Part (3) asks the two slices to be quasi-compact *and quasi-separated over `S`*. Both hold at an
affine object, the slice there being `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.schemeStack` at that object on the
nose: quasi-compactness is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isQuasiCompactStack_schemeStack_of_isAffine` and quasi-separatedness over
`S` is `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.isQuasiSeparatedOver_schemeStack_of_isAffine`, proved in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/QuasiSeparatedSchemeStack.lean`. That file is what starts
the chain: every *other* producer of `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.IsQuasiSeparatedOver` consumes a
quasi-separatedness statement —
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.AlgebraicStacks.isQuasiSeparatedOver_of_isQuasiSeparated`
(**Stacks 050L** part (4)),
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.AlgebraicStacks.isQuasiSeparatedOver_of_isQuasiSeparatedOver_changeBase`
and `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.AlgebraicStacks.isQuasiSeparatedOver_of_isAbsolutelyQuasiSeparated`
(**Stacks 050N** part (4)) among them — so none of those can.

For condition (2)(c) alone part (1) of Stacks 075S would do, since it asks for quasi-compactness
of the source and quasi-separatedness over `S` of the *target* only, and the target there is `𝒳`.
Part (3) is used because condition (4) of Stacks 0GN4 needs the quasi-separatedness of the
2-fibre product that part (3) delivers alongside its quasi-compactness: there the target being
base-changed is a 2-fibre product rather than `𝒳`. See
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Stacks/ColimitsOfCohomologyEqualizerDictionary.lean`.

## Implementation notes

`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.homEquivBase` is the universal property of a strongly
cartesian morphism read as a bijection: surjectivity is the existence of the induced morphism
(`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.map`) and injectivity is its uniqueness
(`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.map_uniq`), while that the induced morphism really
does lie over the given base morphism is `CategoryTheory.IsHomLift.eq_of_isHomLift`. It is stated
for an arbitrary functor with a single strongly cartesian morphism rather than for a category
fibred in groupoids, because nothing else of the fibred structure is used; in a category fibred in
groupoids every morphism is strongly cartesian
(`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFiberedInGroupoids.isStronglyCartesian`), so the hypothesis is
automatic there.

`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.homPairEquivBase` states its condition on the base morphism `c` as a
universally quantified clause over the factorisations lying over `c`, rather than by naming the
factorisation that `homEquivBase` produces. There is exactly one such factorisation, by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.eq_of_map_eq`, so the two readings agree; quantifying
keeps the *statement* free of the strongly-cartesian instance, which otherwise has to be supplied
by hand inside the type, and keeps the hypothesis of
`isLocallySurjective_prodLift_fppfTopology_of_exists_base_hom` readable.

The passage from a covering sieve of the base to a covering singleton upstairs is
`CategoryTheory.Functor.generate_singleton_mem_inheritedTopology`. The fppf topology of
Stacks 06TP is by definition the topology the total category inherits from the localization of the
big fppf topology at `S`, and `AlgebraicGeometry.BigFppfOver.topology` is that localization, so
the conclusion of that lemma is literally a covering sieve of the fppf site and no transport is
needed.

